<h1>Vocab: Composite Modeling from Primitives</h1>
<dl>
<dt>composite model</dt>
<dd>An object assembled at draw time from several primitive shapes (cylinders,
boxes, discs) instead of loaded from a mesh file. Cheap to author, zero asset
dependencies; only suitable for simple convex silhouettes.</dd>

<dt>immediate-mode primitive</dt>
<dd>A shape drawn by a direct API call each frame (<code>DrawCylinder</code>,
<code>DrawCube</code>) rather than retained as a mesh. raylib re-tessellates
per call — fine for a dozen shapes, wasteful for thousands.</dd>

<dt>capped solid</dt>
<dd>What most "cylinder" draw calls actually produce: a closed volume with top
and bottom caps, not a hollow tube. Geometry nested inside is occluded from
above by the object's own cap.</dd>

<dt>curated mapping</dt>
<dd>This codebase's explicit id→model-stem table (<code>curated_</code> in
<code>Assets.cpp</code>) for landmarks that must look a specific way, as
opposed to filler ids that hash into a generic pool.</dd>

<dt>dispatch-before-fallback</dt>
<dd>Ordering rule for lookups with defaults: handle special cases before
consulting the map, because the fallback path cannot distinguish "meant to be
special" from "missing". The fountain check runs before
<code>modelForBuilding</code>.</dd>

<dt>size contract (recap)</dt>
<dd>The <code>SizeSpec</code> table as the only sizing authority: Fill
stretches a model to its collision AABB, Uniform scales it to a declared
world height. Composites obey it too — the fountain reads its height from
the table and its radii from the AABB.</dd>
</dl>
